+# Specialty-Filtered Veterinarian Reads Bypass the Vet Cache
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+`VetRepository` caches its unfiltered reads in the one cache the application declares. The cache is keyed on the method arguments, and `CacheConfiguration` sets no size limit or expiry.
+
+From 2026-09-12, both veterinarian lists can be narrowed by a specialty name taken from the request address (REQ-VET-001, REQ-VET-003). The name is caller-supplied text, and matching ignores letter case. A cached filtered read would key an entry on that text.
+
+The paged unfiltered read is already keyed on the request's page number, which is not clamped. That exposure predates this decision and is recorded in `system-design.md` § Open Questions from the Survey.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Cache the filtered reads alongside the unfiltered ones.** Every distinct name a caller sends, and every letter-case variant of it, becomes a retained entry. Any caller can grow the cache without limit.
+2. **Cache them under a case-folded name.** Case variants collapse to one entry, but every unknown name still adds one.
+3. **Give the cache a size limit.** The limit lives in the cache provider's own configuration, which the project does not set today. The slice would widen into cache infrastructure for every read.
+4. **Leave the filtered reads uncached** (chosen).
+
+## Decision
+
+The specialty-filtered reads carry no cache. The unfiltered reads stay cached as before. A read whose arguments include request-supplied text is not cached while the cache has no size limit.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Each filtered request runs one query against the database. The veterinarian tables are small, and the application never writes them.
+- The filter adds no new way to grow the cache, so the change leaves the application no weaker than the baseline in `security-principles.md` § Realization.
+- Caching a filtered read later requires a bounded cache first, and this ADR is superseded when that lands.
+- The unclamped page-number key stays an open exposure, outside this decision.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-001, REQ-VET-003
+
+- `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/vet/VetRepository.java`: the specialty-filtered reads, declared without a cache.
+- `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/system/CacheConfiguration.java`: the cache declaration, unchanged.
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § Contracts](../system-design.md#contracts) — the `VetRepository` row
+- [system-design.md § Open Questions from the Survey](../system-design.md#open-questions-from-the-survey) — vet cache invalidation and the page-number key
+- [ADR: Filtering the Veterinarian Directory Is In Scope; Free-Text Veterinarian Search Stays Out](2026-09-12-non-goal-narrow-veterinarian-search.md)
